<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>config()</code></td><td>typed settings, validated at start — and it throws in a browser</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>principal.authenticated</code></td><td>whether this caller presented something the server accepted</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>health()</code></td><td>the account of the app this call is in, for whatever is watching it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>log.info(…)</code></td><td>structured logs, under namespaces you turn on one at a time</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`{#if principal.authenticated}<a href="/account">Account</a>{/if}`} />

    <Section title="Settings the app starts with">
        <p>Config is typed and validated at start, so a bad environment fails before the first
        request rather than during one. Anything that must open before traffic arrives, and close
        after it stops, has a place to do it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/configure-the-app')}>Config</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/run-code-at-start-and-stop')}>Lifecycle</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Knowing who is calling">
        <p><code>principal</code> reads the same name on either side, so a template and a handler
        ask the question identically. The raw request is there when you need what it carried.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/know-who-is-calling')}>Auth &amp; principal</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/read-the-incoming-request')}>Request</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Logs, traces and health">
        <p>Logs go under namespaces you turn on one at a time, a trace id follows one request across
        whatever it calls, and health reports the version and uptime under it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/record-what-happened')}>Logging</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/follow-a-request-across-services')}>Tracing</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/tell-a-load-balancer-you-are-healthy')}>Health</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A content security policy with a nonce">
        <p>Per-request, and without breaking the scripts the app ships itself.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/lock-down-what-the-page-may-load')}>CSP</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Knowing the connection dropped">
        <p>A value that says whether the caller can reach the app, so a page can show it, queue
        around it, and recover when it comes back.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/know-when-the-browser-goes-offline')}>Offline</a></p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
